window.docContents[102].resolve({"/Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy/SysML-v2___-a-quantity-model-organized-by-value-representation/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy--SysML-v2___-a-quantity-model-organized-by-value-representation--How-PropertyKindCalculus-takes-the-kind-as-root-instead":{"contents":"PropertyKindCalculus inverts the nesting: axis (A) is the root. A kind-of-property is a\nfirst-class value (KindOfProperty), and a quantity is indexed by its kind —\nQuantity (k : KindOfProperty) — so the kind is fixed before, and independently\nof, any choice of how the value is recorded. Dimension is not the organizing\nprinciple but a forgetful functor \nthat certifies coherence without deciding what is allowed (developed in the\nnext section). Two consequences fall out directly: two kinds that share a\ndimension remain distinct types — so the dimension-1 disambiguation is\nexpressible — and an operation across kinds is a type error rather than a silent\nsuccess. Value representation — the axis SysML v2 puts at the root — is, candidly,\nnot yet specified here; its principled home is an orthogonal index over the\nkind, never a layer the kind hangs beneath.\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint\u0009Why a calculus, not a taxonomy\u0009SysML v2: a quantity model organized by value representation","header":"3.3.2. How PropertyKindCalculus takes the kind as root instead","id":"/Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy/SysML-v2___-a-quantity-model-organized-by-value-representation/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy--SysML-v2___-a-quantity-model-organized-by-value-representation--How-PropertyKindCalculus-takes-the-kind-as-root-instead"}});